    {
      "slug": "va-loan-require-termite-inspection",
      "before": "A VA loan requires a termite inspection if the property is located in an area designated by the Department of Veterans Affairs as having a moderate to very high risk of wood-destroying insect activity. In these mandatory zones, a clear pest inspection report must be submitted before the mortgage can close.",
      "after": "Only in designated zones. A VA loan requires a termite inspection where the property sits in an area the Department of Veterans Affairs designates as moderate to very high risk for wood-destroying insect activity. In those zones a clear pest inspection report must reach the lender before the mortgage can close; outside them it is not required."
    },
    {
      "slug": "homeowners-insurance-cover-failed-sump-pump",
      "before": "Standard homeowners insurance policies typically exclude damage from sump pump failure or water backing up through drains, as these are often considered maintenance issues or forms of flood-related damage. Specialized \"water backup and sump overflow\" endorsements are generally required to cover such incidents, but these are distinct from flood insurance.",
      "after": "Generally no. Standard homeowners policies typically exclude damage from sump pump failure or water backing up through drains, treating them as maintenance issues or as flood-related damage. A separate \"water backup and sump overflow\" endorsement is usually what covers it, and that endorsement is distinct from flood insurance."
    },
    {
      "slug": "reverse-polarity-mean-electrical-inspection",
      "before": "Reverse polarity on an electrical inspection report means that the hot and neutral wires connected to a standard outlet are reversed at the terminal connections. While plugged-in devices may still receive power, this wiring defect keeps internal components energized even when a device is turned off, creating an electric shock hazard for occupants.",
      "after": "Yes, it is a shock hazard. Reverse polarity means the hot and neutral wires at a standard outlet are reversed at the terminal connections. Plugged-in devices still receive power, but internal components stay energised even when the device is switched off, which is what creates the risk to anyone using them."
    }
  ],
